# mcp-tools-list-deterministic-order - repeated tools/list calls against an
# unchanged daemon return the same tools in the same order.
#
# VALIDATES: spec-mcp2026-4-caching-apps AC-6 (two consecutive tools/list calls
# with unchanged registrations return an identical tools array).
#
# MCP 2026-07-28 server/tools: "Servers SHOULD return tools in a deterministic
# order (i.e., the same ordering across requests when the underlying set of
# tools has not changed)." A wobbling order silently defeats both the client
# caching this phase just enabled and the LLM prompt-cache hits that motivate
# the SHOULD -- nothing errors, the cache simply never hits.
#
# The risk is real rather than theoretical: groupCommands builds from two maps,
# and Go randomizes map iteration order on every range. Group order survives
# because the groups are sorted on a unique prefix; action order survives only
# because the command union is deduplicated at its source, which is what makes a
# sort on action name a total order.
#
# tools-order-stable does the comparison in the client because a .ci can only
# match patterns, and Go's RE2 has no backreference, so "these two responses are
# identical" is not expressible as an expectation.
#
# BYTE IDENTITY, NOT NAME IDENTITY (corrected 2026-07-30). The directive used to
# compare only the tool NAME sequence, which is weaker than the AC it stands
# for: AC-6 asks for a byte-identical tools array "including every action enum
# and every description string", and a wobbling action enum leaves every name in
# place. Byte identity was proved at generation level by
# TestToolOrderDeterministic and by nothing at all on the wire, which is where
# the map ranges that build the enums actually reach a client. The directive now
# compares the raw `tools` bytes and prints a digest of them.
#
# PREVENTS: map iteration reaching the wire, and a duplicate command re-entering
# the union and making a tie-break order arbitrary between calls.

stdin=mcp:terminator=EOF_MCP
tools-order-stable 8
EOF_MCP

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 1
				remote 1
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze --mcp $PORT -:stdin=ze-bgp
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze-test mcp --port $PORT:stdin=mcp:timeout=30s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=tools-order stable=true calls=8
# The digest is a SHA-256 prefix over the raw tools array. Asserting its shape
# proves the byte-level comparison ran: the stable line cannot carry one unless
# toolsOrderStable read the array as bytes rather than as a name list. A
# non-empty tool count comes with it, since two empty arrays are byte-identical.
expect=stdout:pattern=tools-order stable=true calls=8 tools=[1-9][0-9]* digest=[0-9a-f]{16}
reject=stdout:contains=stable=false
